`display: contents` removes the element’s own box, but it should **not** prevent its `::before` and `::after` pseudo-elements from generating boxes. The pseudo-elements are treated as part of the element’s contents and are placed in the parent’s box alongside the element’s children.

For example:

```css
.wrapper {
  display: contents;
}

.wrapper::before {
  content: "Before";
}

.wrapper::after {
  content: "After";
}
```

If `content` is omitted, its default value for `::before` and `::after` computes to `none`, so the pseudo-element is not generated:

```css
.wrapper::before {
  content: none; /* or the default equivalent */
}
```

Also, `display: none` differs from `display: contents`: `display: none` prevents the element and its descendants from generating boxes, whereas `display: contents` removes only the element’s own box. The CSS Display specification notes that major browsers may not implement this behavior correctly in all cases.
